    "source": "sharegpt",
    "why": "IS THE MPS CEILING A USABLE QoS KNOB? T8's first draft was a 'matched partition' tier -- gpucores 50 with MPS 50, on T5's balanced load, to make the scheduler's model of the card true. That framing is WRONG and was withdrawn before it ran. Scheduler honesty and oversubscription are the same dial, not two things: honest means reservations, which means the percentages sum to 100, which means no statistical multiplexing and therefore no reason to run MPS at all. There is no configuration that is both matched AND oversubscribed, so 'should the numbers agree' is not a question anyone should be asking. What you can choose is an OVERBOOKING RATIO -- and if HAMi's deviceCoreScaling is set to N, the coherent pairing is MPS at 100 with the scheduler overbooked by the same N, never 1:1. The question that survives is a QoS one: does an MPS ceiling BOUND THE DAMAGE one tenant can do to another? That needs a real aggressor, which T5's balanced load does not have and T11's 90/10 does. So T8 is T11 with both tenants capped at 50%. T11 measured the uncapped case: the aggressor ran 8.47 concurrent against the victim's 1.51 and took the card, and MPS helped the aggressor MORE than the victim (-35.7% against -28.2%). Prediction: the cap binds on the AGGRESSOR, whose demand exceeds 50%, and not on the victim, whose ~25% share is already inside it -- so aggressor throughput falls and victim latency improves beyond T11's. If instead nothing moves, then CUDA_MPS_ACTIVE_THREAD_PERCENTAGE is as inert as nvidia.com/gpucores was, and MPS has NO usable QoS knob at all -- which is the single most consequential negative result still available in this study."
  },
